mermaidascii
============

A Go library that renders Mermaid diagrams as ASCII or Unicode box drawings.

Provenance
----------

This library is a port. Its ancestry, oldest first:

1. mermaid-ascii — https://github.com/AlexanderGrooff/mermaid-ascii
   Copyright (c) 2023 Alexander Grooff, MIT License.
   The original renderer, written in Go. Introduced the grid layout and the
   edge drawing approach that everything below still builds on.

2. beautiful-mermaid — https://github.com/lukilabs/beautiful-mermaid
   Copyright (c) 2026 Craft Docs, MIT License.
   Ported mermaid-ascii from Go to TypeScript and extended it with sequence,
   class and ER diagrams, Unicode box-drawing characters, node shapes and
   configurable spacing.

3. this library
   Copyright (c) 2026 core.equip, MIT License.
   Ports the ASCII renderer of beautiful-mermaid back to Go, as a library
   with no dependencies outside the standard library.

Why a third implementation
--------------------------

mermaid-ascii is a command line tool, not a library: its core lives in
package `cmd` alongside cobra, logrus and a gin web server. Importing its
one exported entry point, `cmd.DiagramFactory`, grows a Go binary from
1.1 MB to 12 MB. It also parses node shapes incorrectly — `B{Decision}`
becomes two nodes rather than one diamond, and `([stadium])`, `[(cylinder)]`
and `{{hexagon}}` keep their raw brackets.

beautiful-mermaid gets those right, but it is TypeScript, and calling it
from Go would mean shipping a JavaScript runtime.

Hence this: the shapes and layout of beautiful-mermaid, in Go, importable,
with the standard library as its only dependency.

Thanks to Alexander Grooff for the foundation and to Craft Docs for the
shapes. Sequence diagram arrow placement follows mermaid-ascii, which
handles it more carefully than the TypeScript port.
